{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Data Binding in the .NET Framework", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 10, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear and use to talk about .Net Framework. ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Talk about principles, concepts, and characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob10re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Would Rather for What is Preferable", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Would rather</span> is an expression we use to talk about what we prefer instead of something else.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Really? I'd rather you use .NET Framework to bind data. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You're right. I'd rather work with .NET Framework from now on. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": " If you are working with sets of data, you'd rather use the complex data binding, which consists in binding controls to more than one data element or even to different binding data types. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Use <span class=\"strong\"> would rather</span> plus the base form of verbs to express what you prefer. Remember that you can also contract would, this is more common in spoken English.</br></br>Follow this structure: </br> Subject + would rather + Verb in base form + Complement </br></br> Example: </br> <span class=\"italic\"> I <span class=\"strong\"> would rather use </span>the .net for data binding.</span><br> <span class=\"italic\"> You<span class=\"strong\">'d rather ask </span>Tim about the data binding type. </span><br><br>  To make a negative statement with would, you place the negation<span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\"> not </span> </span> between <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\"> rather</span> </span> and the verb.<br><br>  Example:<br> <span class=\"italic\"> I'<span class=\"strong\"> d rather</span> <span class=\"strong\"> not</span> bind data to that web site. </span><br><br>  When the preference refers to a different subject, you place that subject after <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\"> rather</span> </span>.<br><br>Example :<br>  <span class=\"italic\"> I'<span class=\"strong\"> d rather</span><span class=\"strong\"> you </span>  bind the data tonight. </span></span>", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Present Perfect versus Simple Past", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use both, <span class=\"strong\"> simple past</span> and <span class=\"strong\"> present perfect</span>, to talk about the past. However, depending on the idea you want to convey, one is more appropriate than the other.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "I have started a new job on data binding.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I worked on that last year, we used the .NET framework to bind texts and other contents.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\"> Simple past </span>is the tense we usually use when we talk about the <span class=\"strong\"> past</span>. Use simple past when you want to refer to events or actions that happened in the past. Simple past is commonly accompanied by a <span class=\"strong\">past time expression</span>, which refers to the specific moment when the action took place.<br><br>Example:<br> <span class=\"italic\"> Last year I <span class=\"strong\"> had </span> to work with data gathering. </span><br><br>  The structure of the simple past is very simple:</br>Subject + Main verb (past form) + Complement</br></br> Remember that the verbs you use in past in the <span class=\"strong\"> affirmative </span>sentence can be<span class=\"strong\"> regular</span> and <span class=\"strong\">irregular</span>.<br><br>The <span class=\"strong\"> present perfect </span>, on the other hand, is a tense that links <span class=\"strong\"> the past </span> and <span class=\"strong\"> the present </span>. This structure is used to recall a past event, or to refer to a fact that has a consequence, or a connection with the present. The moment when the action was done is not important, but the action itself is. <br><br>  The present perfect uses the verb <span class=\"strong\"> to have </span> conjugated in present as an auxiliary verb plus the <span class=\"strong\"> past participle</span> of the main verb. Remember to learn the past participle of the irregular verbs. <br><br>  <span class=\"italic\"> Examples </span> :<br>  <span class=\"italic\"> I <span class=\"strong\"> have worked </span> with .NET twice. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<div class=\"center_text\"><span class=\"strong\"> Irregular verbs </span> </div>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":8,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\"> Base form </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\"> Simple past </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\"> Past participle </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Say\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Said\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Said\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Make\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Made\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Made\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Go\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Went\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Gone\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Take\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Took\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Taken\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Come\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Came\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Come\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"See\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Saw\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Seen\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Know\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Knew\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Known\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border\"}", "type": "table"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "bind", "type": "name"}, {"content": "hold together", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You'd rather use the complex data binding, which consists of binding controls to more than one data element or even to different binding data types.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob10aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "background", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the base color of a graph", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: With .NET you can set properties, such as the control for graphs and images, and the background color and si